--TEST--
a bound datagram socket, a message that carries the endpoint it came from, and a reply addressed with what that message answered — `rule:core-classes/net-one-api-three-transports` and `rule:security/net-address-policy`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Net\Datagram::send, Core\Net\Datagram::receive, Core\Net\Datagram::port, Core\Net\Datagram::close, Core\Net\Datagram\Message::payload, Core\Net\Datagram\Message::host, Core\Net\Datagram\Message::port
// The whole of `Core\Net`'s datagram third, in the order a program meets it.
// Two sockets in one task and nothing deadlocks, which is a property of
// datagrams rather than of luck: a send does not wait for a receiver, so the
// message is already queued by the time the receive asks for it.
var $client = Core\Net::bindDatagram("127.0.0.1", 0);
var $server = Core\Net::bindDatagram("127.0.0.1", 0);

// There is no connect here and no accept. A datagram socket is bound and never
// connected, so `port` is how a program learns where it can be reached, and the
// address it sends to is named at the send instead.
echo "bound: ", ($client->port() > 0 && $server->port() > 0) as string, "\n";

echo "sent: ", $client->send("127.0.0.1", $server->port(), "ping" as bytes, 5s) as string, "\n";

// What arrives is what was sent, whole, carrying the endpoint it came from —
// and the sender is asserted against a port the case never wrote down, so a
// member answering its own socket's address would read plausibly and fail here.
var $heard = $server->receive(64, 5s);
echo "heard: ", $heard->payload() as string, "\n";
echo "from: ", $heard->host(), " ", ($heard->port() == $client->port()) as string, "\n";

// The reply, addressed with nothing but what the message answered. This is what
// `host` being a plain `string` buys: `send`'s host is a sink, so an answer
// carrying a qualifier could not be handed back to it at all, and a datagram
// socket that cannot reply is most of a datagram socket missing.
$server->send($heard->host(), $heard->port(), "pong" as bytes, 5s);
var $back = $client->receive(64, 5s);
echo "back: ", $back->payload() as string, "\n";

// Closing gives the port and the reactor registration back, and a second close
// throws rather than succeeding quietly: a key is never reused, so this can
// only be the same handle twice.
$client->close();
$server->close();
try {
    $server->close();
    echo "closed twice", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $gone) {
    echo $gone->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# Three keys, exactly as the TCP round trip needs them: binding and reaching out
# are separate powers, and the loopback address this program supplies is one
# `rule:security/net-address-policy` denies by default. `listen = true` rather
# than an endpoint literal, because the program asks for port `0` and there is
# no endpoint a reviewer could have written down in advance.
[capabilities.net]
listen = true
connect = ["127.0.0.1"]
internal = ["127.0.0.1"]
--EXPECT--
bound: 1
sent: 4
heard: ping
from: 127.0.0.1 1
back: pong
Core\Net\Datagram::close: this handle is closed
